export default {"id":"m4l-level-7","number":7,"title":"Module 7","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"7.1","modules":[{"id":"m4l-m-7-1-1","code":"7.1","title":"7.1","blurb":"7.1","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"m4l-l-7-1-1","title":"7.1.1: Complete Device — Stereo Utility Audio Effect","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Metadata and Prerequisites","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Lesson ID:</strong> Lesson 7.1.1","<strong>Module / Section:</strong> Module 7 / Section 7.1","<strong>Track:</strong> <code>m4l-devices</code>","<strong>Target Environment:</strong> Ableton Live 12 Standard (with Max for Live add-on) / Live 12 Suite; bundled Cycling '74 Max 9 runtime.","<strong>Operating Systems:</strong> Windows, macOS.","<strong>Prerequisites:</strong> Lesson 6.4.1 - Presets, Initial Values, and Set Recall.","<strong>Primary Sources:</strong>","Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual, Chapter 31 (<em>Max for Live</em>).","Ableton Production Guidelines: <em>Complete Audio Device Specifications</em>.","Ableton Patch Code Standard: <em>Multi-Instance Safety and Gain Staging</em>."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"By the conclusion of this module, the student will be able to:"},{"type":"list","items":["Design and construct a production-ready, multi-feature <strong>Stereo Utility Audio Effect</strong> from scratch.","Implement independent Left/Right polarity inversion switches using signal multiplication (<code>*~ -1.0</code>).","Implement stereo width management and mono-summing algorithms.","Integrate real-time peak and RMS metering using <code>live.meter~</code> and <code>live.gain~</code>.","Apply complete parameter hygiene (naming, units, visibility, annotations, initial values) and verify multi-instance independence."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Architectural and Theoretical Foundations","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"3.1 Signal Flow Topology","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"The Stereo Utility Device processes incoming audio through four discrete, sequential stages:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                        STEREO UTILITY SIGNAL TOPOLOGY                         |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                                                               |\n|  [ plugin~ ] (Stereo Audio In)                                                |\n|       |                                                                       |\n|       v                                                                       |\n|  [ Stage 1: Polarity Inversion ] (Phase Invert L / R via *~ -1.0)              |\n|       |                                                                       |\n|       v                                                                       |\n|  [ Stage 2: Stereo Width &amp; Mono Fold ] (Mid/Side processing or width matrix)  |\n|       |                                                                       |\n|       v                                                                       |\n|  [ Stage 3: Calibrated Master Gain ] (line~ smoothed attenuation/boost)       |\n|       |                                                                       |\n|       +------------------------------------+                                  |\n|       |                                    |                                  |\n|       v                                    v                                  |\n|  [ Stage 4: Visual Metering ]         [ plugout~ ] (Stereo Audio Out)         |\n|  (live.meter~ / live.gain~)                                                   |\n|                                                                               |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"heading","text":"3.2 Polarity Inversion (Phase Inversion) Mathematics","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"Inverting the acoustic phase of a channel requires multiplying the audio signal vector by -1.0:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                   [ Audio Signal ]\n                          |\n                          v\n                    +-----------+\n                    |    *~     |&lt;==== [ Invert Switch: +1.0 or -1.0 ]\n                    +-----+-----+\n                          |\n                          v\n                 [ Inverted Signal ]</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["When Invert is <strong>Off (0)</strong> → Multiplier receives <code>+1.0</code> (Unity passthrough).","When Invert is <strong>On (1)</strong> → Multiplier receives <code>-1.0</code> (180^ polarity inversion)."],"ordered":false},{"type":"heading","text":"3.3 Stereo Width Matrix","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"Stereo width adjustment splits stereo (L, R) into Mid (M) and Side (S) components:"},{"type":"prose","html":"M = L + R2, S = L - R2"},{"type":"prose","html":"Recombining with a width scalar W [0.0, 2.0]:"},{"type":"prose","html":"L_out = M + (W × S), R_out = M - (W × S)"},{"type":"list","items":["W = 0.0 → Pure Mono fold.","W = 1.0 → 100% Original Stereo.","W = 2.0 → 200% Expanded Stereo."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"4. Complete Device Implementation Specification","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"4.1 Parameter Registry","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                            DEVICE PARAMETER SPECIFICATION                         |\n+------------------+------------+------------+-------+------------+--------+--------+\n| Long Name        | Short Name | Scripting  | Type  | Unit Style | Range  | Initial|\n+------------------+------------+------------+-------+------------+--------+--------+\n| Master_Gain      | Gain       | mst_gain   | Float | Decibel    | -70/+12| 0.0 dB |\n| Stereo_Width     | Width      | str_width  | Float | Percentage | 0-200% | 100.0% |\n| Invert_Left      | Inv L      | inv_l      | Enum  | On/Off     | 0 - 1  | 0 (Off)|\n| Invert_Right     | Inv R      | inv_r      | Enum  | On/Off     | 0 - 1  | 0 (Off)|\n| Mute_Output      | Mute       | out_mute   | Enum  | On/Off     | 0 - 1  | 0 (Off)|\n+------------------+------------+------------+-------+------------+--------+--------+</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"5. Laboratory Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Step-by-Step Construction Procedure","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["In Ableton Live 12, insert a <strong>Max Audio Effect</strong> on an Audio Track and select <strong>Edit in Max</strong>.","Open Patcher Inspector → Enable <strong>Open in Presentation</strong>.","Create Audio I/O: <code>[plugin~]</code> and <code>[plugout~]</code>.","Implement Polarity Inversion:","Create two <code>[live.toggle]</code> controls: <code>Invert_Left</code> and <code>Invert_Right</code>.","Route <code>Invert_Left</code> through a <code>[select 0 1]</code> object:","<code>0</code> emits message <code>1.0</code> → <code>[sig~ 1.0]</code>.","<code>1</code> emits message <code>-1.0</code> → <code>[sig~ -1.0]</code>.","Multiply Left audio from <code>[plugin~]</code> with the invert signal using <code>[*~ 1.0]</code>.","Replicate identical logic for Right audio.","Implement Master Gain &amp; Mute:","Create <code>[live.dial Master_Gain]</code>. Route to <code>[dbtoa]</code> (Decibels to Amplitude) → <code>[1 20(</code>\\rightarrow$ <code>[line~ 1.0]</code>.","Multiply Left and Right channels by the gain signal.","Connect <code>[live.meter~]</code> to the final stereo outputs for real-time visualization.","Connect the final outputs to <code>[plugout~]</code>.","Add all user controls to Presentation Mode and arrange them cleanly on an 8x8 pixel grid.","Save the device as <code>Production_Stereo_Utility.amxd</code> in <code>User Library/Presets/Audio Effects/Max Audio Effect/</code>.","Verify in Live across multiple tracks: Confirm independent operation with zero cross-talk."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"6. Common Pitfalls and Failure Modes","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Unsmoothed Gain Changes on Width:</strong> Modulating width dynamically without smoothing the Mid/Side multipliers, resulting in zipper noise during automation.","<strong>Missing <code>dbtoa</code> Conversion:</strong> Routing a Decibel gain dial (-70 to +12) directly into <code>[*~]</code>. A multiplier expecting amplitude (0.0 - 1.0) will treat +12 as a massive +1200% volume boost, causing dangerous acoustic overload. Always convert dB to amplitude using <code>[dbtoa]</code>.","<strong>Phase Invert Click:</strong> Switching polarity instantly at high signal amplitudes. Polarity toggles should be used primarily for static correction or smoothed with a brief ramp."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"7. Self-Assessment & Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Checkpoint Criteria","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"To confirm mastery of this module, the student must successfully complete the following criteria:"},{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Construct the complete Mid/Side width matrix and demonstrate mono collapse at 0% width.","[ ] Verify that reversing phase on one channel of an identical mono signal causes complete phase cancellation when summed.","[ ] Confirm that two instances of the device running on separate tracks in Live operate with complete parameter independence."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"8. Source Citations","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton AG. (2024). <em>Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual</em>, Chapter 31: \"Max for Live\", pp. 581–588. Berlin: Ableton AG.","Ableton AG. (2023). <em>Max for Live Production Guidelines: Audio Device Checklist</em>. Ableton Developer Documentation.","Cycling '74. (2024). <em>MSP Reference Manual: dbtoa and Signal Math</em>. San Francisco: Cycling '74."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"m4l-m-7-2-2","code":"7.2","title":"7.2","blurb":"7.2","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"m4l-l-7-2-1","title":"7.2.1: Complete Device — MIDI Processing Utility","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Metadata and Prerequisites","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Lesson ID:</strong> Lesson 7.2.1","<strong>Module / Section:</strong> Module 7 / Section 7.2","<strong>Track:</strong> <code>m4l-devices</code>","<strong>Target Environment:</strong> Ableton Live 12 Standard (with Max for Live add-on) / Live 12 Suite; bundled Cycling '74 Max 9 runtime.","<strong>Operating Systems:</strong> Windows, macOS.","<strong>Prerequisites:</strong> Lesson 7.1.1 - Complete Device — Stereo Utility Audio Effect.","<strong>Primary Sources:</strong>","Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual, Section 31.3 (<em>Creating MIDI Effects</em>).","Cycling '74: <em>Creating MIDI Effect Devices in Max for Live</em>.","Ableton Patch Code Standard: <em>MIDI Flow and Stream Hygiene</em>."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"By the conclusion of this module, the student will be able to:"},{"type":"list","items":["Design and build a production-grade <strong>MIDI Utility Processor</strong> combining note filtering, pitch transposition, velocity curvature, and CC filtering.","Implement bounded note range filtering using <code>split</code> and <code>stripnote</code> logic.","Apply non-linear velocity curve transfer functions (Linear, Exponential, Logarithmic) to shape keyboard dynamics.","Integrate a continuous controller (CC) filter with an automatable bypass toggle.","Verify MIDI message stream integrity using Live's built-in MIDI Monitor tools."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Architectural and Theoretical Foundations","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"3.1 Device Processing Topology","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"The MIDI Utility Processor intercepts the live MIDI stream, segregates note events from control data, applies transformation pipelines, and recombines the streams into <code>[midiout]</code>:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                         MIDI UTILITY SIGNAL TOPOLOGY                          |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                                                               |\n|  [ midiin ] (Raw Live MIDI Stream)                                            |\n|       |                                                                       |\n|       v                                                                       |\n|  [ midiparse ] (Splits stream into Notes, CC, Pitch Bend, Aftertouch, Program)|\n|       |                                         |                             |\n|       | (Note Stream)                           | (CC / Other Streams)        |\n|       v                                         v                             |\n|  +---------------------------+            +---------------------------+       |\n|  |  Stage 1: Note Range Map  |            |  Stage 4: CC Filter / Pass|       |\n|  |  (Low Key / High Key Split|            |  (Allow / Block CC toggle)|       |\n|  +-------------+-------------+            +-------------+-------------+       |\n|                |                                        |                     |\n|                v                                        |                     |\n|  +---------------------------+                          |                     |\n|  |  Stage 2: Pitch Transpose |                          |                     |\n|  |  (+/- 36 Semitones)       |                          |                     |\n|  +-------------+-------------+                          |                     |\n|                |                                        |                     |\n|                v                                        |                     |\n|  +---------------------------+                          |                     |\n|  |  Stage 3: Velocity Curve  |                          |                     |\n|  |  (Lin / Exp / Log Reshape)|                          |                     |\n|  +-------------+-------------+                          |                     |\n|                |                                        |                     |\n|                +--------------------+-------------------+                     |\n|                                     |                                         |\n|                                     v                                         |\n|                               [ midiformat ]                                  |\n|                                     |                                         |\n|                                     v                                         |\n|                               [ midiout ] (Processed MIDI to Live)            |\n|                                                                               |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"heading","text":"3.2 Velocity Curve Mathematics","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"To modify velocity dynamics musically without clipping, incoming velocities V_in [1, 127] are normalized to the range [0.0, 1.0]:"},{"type":"prose","html":"v_norm = V_in127.0"},{"type":"prose","html":"Applying an exponential curvature coefficient k &gt; 0:"},{"type":"prose","html":"v_curved = (v_norm)^k × 127.0"},{"type":"list","items":["k = 1.0 → Linear Response (Direct passthrough).","k &lt; 1.0 → Logarithmic Response (Boosts soft notes; high sensitivity).","k &gt; 1.0 → Exponential Response (Suppresses soft notes; requires hard playing)."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"4. Device Parameter Specification","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                            DEVICE PARAMETER SPECIFICATION                         |\n+------------------+------------+------------+-------+------------+--------+--------+\n| Long Name        | Short Name | Scripting  | Type  | Unit Style | Range  | Initial|\n+------------------+------------+------------+-------+------------+--------+--------+\n| Pitch_Transpose  | Transpose  | p_trans    | Int   | Semitone   | -36/+36| 0 st   |\n| Min_Note_Limit   | Min Key    | min_key    | Int   | MIDI Note  | 0 - 127| 0 (C-2)|\n| Max_Note_Limit   | Max Key    | max_key    | Int   | MIDI Note  | 0 - 127| 127(G8)|\n| Velocity_Curve   | Vel Curve  | vel_curve  | Float | Float      | 0.2-3.0| 1.0    |\n| Block_All_CC     | Block CC   | blk_cc     | Enum  | On/Off     | 0 - 1  | 0 (Off)|\n+------------------+------------+------------+-------+------------+--------+--------+</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"5. Laboratory Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Step-by-Step Construction Procedure","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["In Ableton Live 12, insert a <strong>Max MIDI Effect</strong> before an instrument on a MIDI Track.","Select <strong>Edit in Max</strong> and enable <strong>Open in Presentation</strong> in the Patcher Inspector.","Ingest raw MIDI using <code>[midiin]</code>, route to <code>[midiparse]</code>.","Implement Note Processing:","Extract pitch and velocity from <code>[midiparse]</code> outlet 1.","Use <code>[split 0 127]</code> to filter notes falling between <code>Min_Note_Limit</code> and <code>Max_Note_Limit</code>.","Add <code>Pitch_Transpose</code> to the filtered pitch using <code>[+ 0]</code>.","Normalize velocity → apply <code>[pow]</code> with <code>Velocity_Curve</code> → scale back to 1–127 with <code>[clip 1 127]</code>.","Implement CC Filtering:","Route <code>[midiparse]</code> outlet 2 (CC) through a <code>[gate]</code> object controlled by <code>Block_All_CC</code>.","Reconstruct MIDI: Route processed note tuples, pitch bend, and CC through <code>[midiformat]</code> → <code>[midiout]</code>.","Add all UI controls to Presentation Mode and align on the 8x8 pixel grid.","Save the device as <code>Production_MIDI_Utility.amxd</code> in <code>User Library/Presets/MIDI Effects/Max MIDI Effect/</code>.","Insert Live's <strong>MIDI Monitor</strong> device downstream. Verify that playing notes outside the configured range are blocked, and velocity curves reshape dynamics predictably."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"6. Common Pitfalls and Failure Modes","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Filtering Note-Off Out-of-Range:</strong> If a user presses a key inside the valid range, adjusts the <code>Min Key</code> dial above that note while holding the key, and then releases it, the Note-Off event will be blocked, causing a stuck note. Use <code>[stripnote]</code> and pitch storage buffers for advanced edge-case isolation.","<strong>Dropping Pitch Bend and Modulation:</strong> Wiring only the note outlets of <code>[midiparse]</code> to <code>[midiformat]</code>. This strips mod wheels, pitch bend, and sustain pedals from the stream. Always connect non-note outlets through passthrough cables.","<strong>Double MIDI Event Generation:</strong> Connecting both raw <code>[midiin] -&gt; [midiout]</code> and processed streams simultaneously, doubling every note event."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"7. Self-Assessment & Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Checkpoint Criteria","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"To confirm mastery of this module, the student must successfully complete the following criteria:"},{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Construct the mathematical velocity curvature subpatch and verify output values for velocities 32, 64, and 127 at curve settings k = 0.5, k = 1.0, and k = 2.0.","[ ] Demonstrate complete CC suppression when <code>Block_All_CC</code> is engaged without affecting pitch bend or note playback.","[ ] Confirm zero hanging notes when playing rapid legato passages across keyboard split boundaries."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"8. Source Citations","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton AG. (2024). <em>Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual</em>, Section 31.3: \"Creating MIDI Effects\", pp. 585–586. Berlin: Ableton AG.","Cycling '74. (2024). <em>Max 9 Reference Manual: midiparse and midiformat</em>. San Francisco: Cycling '74.","Ableton AG. (2023). <em>Ableton Patch Code Standard: MIDI Integrity Guidelines</em>. Ableton Developer Documentation."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"m4l-m-7-3-3","code":"7.3","title":"7.3","blurb":"7.3","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"m4l-l-7-3-1","title":"7.3.1: Complete Device — Monophonic Synthesizer","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Metadata and Prerequisites","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Lesson ID:</strong> Lesson 7.3.1 (Includes Gate F Assessment)","<strong>Module / Section:</strong> Module 7 / Section 7.3","<strong>Track:</strong> <code>m4l-devices</code>","<strong>Target Environment:</strong> Ableton Live 12 Standard (with Max for Live add-on) / Live 12 Suite; bundled Cycling '74 Max 9 runtime.","<strong>Operating Systems:</strong> Windows, macOS.","<strong>Prerequisites:</strong> Lesson 7.2.1 - Complete Device — MIDI Processing Utility.","<strong>Primary Sources:</strong>","Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual, Section 31.3 (<em>Creating Instruments</em>).","Cycling '74: <em>Creating Audio Devices: Instruments in Max for Live</em>.","Ableton Production Guidelines: <em>Instrument Architecture and Headroom</em>."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"By the conclusion of this module, the student will be able to:"},{"type":"list","items":["Design and assemble a fully realized <strong>Monophonic Subtractive Synthesizer</strong> instrument with multi-waveform selection, resonant filtering, ADSR envelope shaping, and output limiting.","Implement low-latency multi-waveform oscillator generation using <code>cycle~</code>, <code>saw~</code>, and <code>rect~</code>.","Construct a 4-stage ADSR amplitude envelope with velocity scaling.","Integrate a resonant lowpass filter with keyboard tracking and dedicated envelope modulation.","Complete <strong>Gate F (First Shippable Trio)</strong> by completing, testing, and archiving a full suite of three foundational devices (Audio Utility, MIDI Utility, Monophonic Synth)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Architectural and Theoretical Foundations","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"3.1 Synthesis Signal Topology","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"The Monophonic Synthesizer converts MIDI note streams into shaped audio waveforms via four interconnected synthesis blocks:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                       MONOPHONIC SYNTHESIZER TOPOLOGY                         |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                                                               |\n|  [ notein ] (MIDI Pitch &amp; Velocity)                                           |\n|       |                                                                       |\n|       +-----------------------+-----------------------+                       |\n|       | (Pitch)               | (Gate / Velocity)     | (Keyboard Tracking)   |\n|       v                       v                       v                       |\n|  [ mtof ]               [ adsr~ Env ]           [ Key Track Scaling ]         |\n|       |                       |                       |                       |\n|       v                       |                       v                       |\n|  [ Oscillator Engine ]        |                 [ svf~ Filter Engine ]        |\n|  (cycle~ / saw~ / rect~)      |                       ^                       |\n|       |                       |                       |                       |\n|       +=======================+=======================+                       |\n|                               | (Filtered Audio)                              |\n|                               v                                               |\n|                         [ *~ VCA Stage ] &lt;==== [ adsr~ Amp Envelope ]         |\n|                               |                                               |\n|                               v                                               |\n|                         [ Master Gain &amp; Headroom Limiter ]                    |\n|                               |                                               |\n|                               v                                               |\n|                         [ plugout~ ] (Stereo Audio to Live)                   |\n|                                                                               |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"heading","text":"3.2 Dynamic Waveform Generation","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"The oscillator stage provides three primary waveforms switchable via an enumerated <code>live.tab</code>:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Sine (<code>[cycle~]</code>):</strong> Pure fundamental tone; zero harmonic overtones.","<strong>Sawtooth (<code>[saw~]</code>):</strong> Rich, even and odd harmonic series (1/n amplitude decay).","<strong>Square / Pulse (<code>[rect~]</code>):</strong> Odd harmonic series (1/n amplitude decay for 50% duty cycle)."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                 [ Waveform Select Index: 0, 1, 2 ]\n                                 |\n                                 v\n                       +-------------------+\n                       |    selector~ 3    |\n                       +---+-----+-----+---+\n                           |     |     |\n               [cycle~] ===+     |     +=== [rect~]\n                                 |\n                            [saw~]\n                                 |\n                                 v (Selected Audio Waveform)</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"4. Complete Parameter Specification","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                            DEVICE PARAMETER SPECIFICATION                         |\n+------------------+------------+------------+-------+------------+--------+--------+\n| Long Name        | Short Name | Scripting  | Type  | Unit Style | Range  | Initial|\n+------------------+------------+------------+-------+------------+--------+--------+\n| Oscillator_Wave  | Wave       | osc_wave   | Enum  | Sine/Saw/Sq| 0 - 2  | 1 (Saw)|\n| Filter_Cutoff    | Cutoff     | flt_cutoff | Float | Hertz      | 20-18k | 2000 Hz|\n| Filter_Resonance | Reso       | flt_reso   | Float | Linear     | 0.0-0.9| 0.2    |\n| Envelope_Attack  | Attack     | env_att    | Float | Time (ms)  | 1-5000 | 10 ms  |\n| Envelope_Decay   | Decay      | env_dec    | Float | Time (ms)  | 1-5000 | 250 ms |\n| Envelope_Sustain | Sustain    | env_sus    | Float | Percentage | 0-100% | 70 %   |\n| Envelope_Release | Release    | env_rel    | Float | Time (ms)  | 1-5000 | 300 ms |\n| Master_Volume    | Volume     | mst_vol    | Float | Decibel    | -70/+6 | -6.0 dB|\n+------------------+------------+------------+-------+------------+--------+--------+</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"5. Laboratory Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Step-by-Step Construction Procedure","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["In Ableton Live 12, create a MIDI Track, insert a <strong>Max Instrument</strong>, and select <strong>Edit in Max</strong>.","Enable <strong>Open in Presentation</strong> in the Patcher Inspector.","Ingest MIDI: Route <code>[notein]</code> pitch outlet through <code>[mtof]</code>. Route frequency float into <code>[cycle~]</code>, <code>[saw~]</code>, and <code>[rect~]</code>.","Waveform Selection: Connect the three oscillator outlets into inputs 1, 2, and 3 of <code>[selector~ 3]</code>. Connect <code>[live.tab Oscillator_Wave]</code> (offset +1) to the selector control inlet.","Filter Stage: Route selected audio through <code>[svf~]</code>. Connect <code>[live.dial Filter_Cutoff]</code> and <code>[live.dial Filter_Resonance]</code> to inlets 2 and 3 with <code>[line~]</code> smoothing.","Envelope &amp; VCA:","Route <code>[notein]</code> velocity through <code>[&gt; 0]</code> gate to <code>[adsr~ 10 250 0.7 300]</code>.","Connect Attack, Decay, Sustain, Release <code>live.dial</code> controls to <code>[adsr~]</code> configuration inlets.","Multiply Lowpass filter output by <code>[adsr~]</code> envelope using <code>[*~ 0.0]</code>.","Master Stage: Route enveloped audio through <code>[live.dial Master_Volume]</code> → <code>[dbtoa]</code> → <code>[*~ 0.5]</code> headroom limiter → <code>[plugout~]</code>.","Layout &amp; Aesthetics: Add all UI widgets to Presentation Mode, set typography to <strong>Ableton Sans</strong>, align to 8x8 grid.","Save device as <code>Production_Mono_Synth.amxd</code> in <code>User Library/Presets/Instruments/Max Instrument/</code>.","Play from Live MIDI clips: Confirm crisp articulation, resonant filter shaping, smooth decay envelopes, and zero console warnings."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"6. Gate F Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"callout","variant":"key","title":"Note","html":"[!IMPORTANT] <strong>Gate F: First Shippable Trio Assessment</strong> To complete Module 7 and achieve full foundational builder status, verify that all three complete devices meet 100% of production standards:"},{"type":"heading","text":"Verification Audit:","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>The Audio Utility (<code>Production_Stereo_Utility.amxd</code>):</strong>","Independent polarity inversion on Left and Right channels.","True stereo Mid/Side width processing from mono (0%) to wide (200%).","Multi-instance tested: Two instances on separate tracks operate with zero cross-talk.","<strong>The MIDI Utility (<code>Production_MIDI_Utility.amxd</code>):</strong>","Note range filtering blocks out-of-range notes without hanging Note-Off events.","Velocity dynamics reshape smoothly across Exponential and Logarithmic curves.","<strong>The Monophonic Synthesizer (<code>Production_Mono_Synth.amxd</code>):</strong>","Accurate equal-temperament pitch response across 8 octaves.","Velocity-sensitive ADSR envelope and resonant lowpass filter.","Zero audio clipping at nominal volume levels.","<strong>Universal Criteria:</strong> All three devices open exclusively in Presentation Mode, follow Live 12 themes dynamically, pass the Blind Reopen Test in <code>.als</code> sets, and output zero console errors on load."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"7. Common Pitfalls and Failure Modes","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Missing Headroom Gain Attenuation:</strong> Allowing raw resonant oscillator signals to pass unattenuated into <code>[plugout~]</code>. Highly resonant filter peaks will clip violently above 0 dBFS. Always maintain 6–12 dB of internal headroom.","<strong>Envelope Click on Rapid Retriggering:</strong> Using an Attack time of 0 ms. Instantaneous step functions in amplitude cause audible clicks on note onset. Maintain a minimum Attack time of 1–2 ms.","<strong>Selector Offset Indexing Error:</strong> Routing a 0-indexed <code>live.tab</code> (0, 1, 2) directly into <code>[selector~ 3]</code>. <code>selector~</code> expects 1-based indexing (1, 2, 3), where 0 = Mute All. Always add +1 to <code>live.tab</code> indices."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"8. Source Citations","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton AG. (2024). <em>Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual</em>, Section 31.3: \"Creating Instruments\", pp. 586–587. Berlin: Ableton AG.","Cycling '74. (2024). <em>MSP Reference Manual: selector~, adsr~, and svf~</em>. San Francisco: Cycling '74.","Ableton AG. (2023). <em>Ableton Patch Code Standard: Instrument Design &amp; Voice Topology</em>. Ableton Developer Documentation."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
